1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,537
Why Do We Have Birthdays?

2
00:00:02,487 --> 00:00:07,637
Ancient Egyptians celebrated pharaohs' coronation days as divine birthdays.

3
00:00:07,637 --> 00:00:13,625
Greeks added candles to cakes as glowing offerings to Artemis, meant to carry wishes to the gods.

4
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:21,075
Birthday celebrations for ordinary people became common only in the 19th century, driven by rising middle-class prosperity.

5
00:00:21,075 --> 00:00:28,950
The song Happy Birthday, written in 1893 by Patty and Mildred Hill, is now the most recognized English-language song.

6
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:40,737
Traditions vary widely: Mexicans strike pinatas, Koreans eat miyeok-guk (seaweed soup), and many African cultures have adopted birthday celebrations through global cultural exchange.

7
00:00:40,737 --> 00:00:49,062
The cake, candles, and gift-giving rituals that seem universal today are actually a relatively modern convergence of ancient traditions.

